"""Research-note data only. Never edits the frozen transcription."""
ITEMS=[]
def add(id, section, title, scan, location, kind, body, context, ids, terms, confidence=''):
    ITEMS.append(dict(id=id,section=section,title=title,scan=scan,location=location,kind=kind,body=body,context=context,ids=ids,terms=terms,confidence=confidence))
H1='Hua finding 137-perfect-complexes-universal-sigma-exact (candidate; not independently reviewed)'
H2='Hua finding 137-polynomial-categories-pd-tensor-categories (candidate; not independently reviewed)'
WG='WG2 §Week #3 (unchecked plan)'
B6='MPL_133 B06 (first-pass lead)'

add('A01','Universal complexes and relative exactness','The universal evaluation argument is local to additive generators','045','middle–bottom; TeX lines 10–47','unpursued implication',r'''The proof reduces the evaluation map to the displayed additive generators and ends “va à l’identité, qfd.”, while the generator superscript remains uncertain in the frozen reading. This is an additive-generation/Yoneda mechanism; applying it to a larger completion would require checking that the relevant sums, products or retracts are still controlled. It supplies a precise mechanism for recovering a functor from its universal complex, without yet recovering a space.''',r'''Context: this restates the argument in Hua, modernised reading of cote 137, “Les réalisations de $\mathbb K$” (our 045), and adds a scope boundary to its universal-property finding.''',H1,r'évaluation|universel|réalisations|représentab')
add('A02','Universal complexes and relative exactness','Full faithfulness and the Karoubian hypothesis','046–047','046 diagrams;047 lines 30–40','unpursued implication',r'''The assertion “Si $B$ est karoubienne” separates full faithfulness from essential surjectivity in the Dold–Puppe realization. The hypothesis should remain attached to Proposition 5 when comparing models or changing the target category. The notation “½ simplicial” should therefore be compared with the actual Dold–Puppe functors, rather than translated solely by the present-day word semisimplicial.''',r'''Context: this supports Hua, “Conventions, valables pour tout le dossier” and “Les réalisations de $\mathbb K$”; PS VII §134, pp. 558–559, adds a separate warning that an unnormalised integrator functor is not itself the Kan–Dold–Puppe equivalence.''',H1+'; '+WG,r'Dold|karoub|normalis|134')
add('A03','Universal complexes and relative exactness','The initial exactness axioms are not yet the full exact-category package','049–050;060–061','049 Sex1–Sex2;050 lines23–24;060 condition d)','own idea / comparison',r'''The initial “Sex 1)” and “Sex 2)” give kernel–cokernel pairs, isomorphism invariance, finite sums and split sequences, while composition is explicitly queried on 050 and pushouts/pullbacks are added on 060. A modern exact-category reading must specify the stage of the argument and verify the remaining closure requirements; it cannot silently backdate all Quillen axioms to 049. Bühler [B09, Definition 2.1] provides a precise modern checklist for this comparison.''',r'''Context: this sharpens Hua, “Les définitions” and “Les conditions a) à e)”, which already identify the missing initial axioms; it leaves the candidate universal theorem conditional on its actual assumptions.''',H1,r'Sex|Quillen|pushout|somme amalgam|compos', 'High for the distinction of axiom lists; no new exact-category theorem is claimed.')
add('A04','Universal complexes and relative exactness','The opening theorem is revised and subsequently argued','051–056','051 lines3–43;053–056 proof','withdrawn in the same text / self-flagged',r'''The lead-in “Il faudrait prouver” is followed by a deleted equivalence and the replacement “est pleinement fidèle”, together with an essential-image condition. The adjunction and finite-length argument on 053–056 address this opening uncertainty, so it would be misleading either to call the entire result unproved or to erase its qualifications. The exact-functor part retains the narrower abelian qualification recorded in A07.''',r'''Context: this adds the sequence of revisions to Hua, “Le théorème”, and to the truncated candidate statement in RESULTS_STATE; neither contextual summary substitutes for the proof and caveats in the scans.''',H1,r'Il faudrait|pleinement fidèle|équivalence|Le théorème')
add('A05','Universal complexes and relative exactness','The flatness question has a narrower target than all later multiplication','053–056','053 margin line23;055 induction;056 NB','reading question / unpursued implication',r'''The margin asks “non, il faut condition de platitude ?” next to the additive adjunction. The finite-free source complexes and the explicit pullback induction on 055 suggest a route to the left-exact reconstruction without an extra tensor-flatness assumption, but that observation does not answer every later multiplicative extension question. For $\sigma$ to take values in $B$, the required kernels must exist in $B$; the presheaf-valued workaround on 056 is a separate part of the full-faithfulness argument.''',r'''Context: Hua, “Le théorème” and its Lean footnote, makes the additive full-faithfulness claim and explicitly limits the reported formalization; this note agrees with that limited scope and differs from any inference that all of 051–071 has thereby been verified.''',H1,r'platitude|ρFullyFaithful|noyaux|présheaf')
add('A06','Universal complexes and relative exactness','The induction uses a controlled pullback presentation','055–056','055 lines17–46;056 lines3–9','unpursued implication',r'''After checking the unit on $C^i$ and $\Psi^i$, the proof writes a bounded complex as a “produit fibré” involving a shorter truncation and an elementary complex. This gives a practical induction for a precise modern proof: establish the presentation, identify the displayed epimorphism as admissible, and apply the specified left-exact functors. It does not require simply declaring all extensions split as complexes.''',r'''Context: this restates and isolates the key step of Hua, “Démonstration” within “Le théorème”; it adds a proof obligation useful for checking the universal-property candidate.''',H1,r'produit fibré|récurrence|tronqué|induct')
add('A07','Universal complexes and relative exactness','The abelian qualification must survive a modern statement','056','bottom;lines28–33','reading question / scope of claim',r'''The assertion that exact functors correspond to resolutions ends “du moins si $B$ cat. abélienne avec $\Sigma$ ttes les suites exactes…”. A formulation for arbitrary additive categories with a chosen exactness class is therefore stronger than the unqualified part of this page. Its proof would have to show the exact-functor/resolution equivalence under those weaker axioms, rather than cite the additive adjunction alone.''',r'''Context: this differs from Hua, “Le théorème”, part b and its concluding proof paragraph, which state the equivalence without retaining this restriction; its reported Lean scope explicitly excludes that part.''',H1,r'du moins|résolution|partie b|foncteurs exacts')
add('A08','Universal complexes and relative exactness','Finite duality does not require reflexivity of the arbitrary test complex','056–057','056 example;057 full text','own idea',r'''The example calls the formula $\operatorname{Hom}(\lambda,L)\simeq\operatorname{Hom}(L^\vee,\lambda^\vee)$ “élémentaire”. For bounded finite-free $L$, transposition uses the reflexivity of $L$, not a claim that an arbitrary $\lambda$ is reflexive; degreewise compatibility with the differential then gives the displayed chain-map correspondence. This is a useful check against inadvertently adding a finite-type assumption to the variable $\lambda$.''',r'''Context: this restates Hua, final example of “Le théorème”, and adds an explanation of which variable carries the finite-duality hypothesis.''',H1,r'lambda|check|dualité|élémentaire','High: the tensor–Hom adjunction for a finite-free target gives the inverse explicitly; this is a standard calculation, not a novelty claim.')
add('A09','Universal complexes and relative exactness','The intrinsic exactness condition is a serious reading boundary','058','bottom;lines62–78','reading question',r'''The clause beginning “Les suites” is heavily revised and remains uncertain in the frozen transcription, including the projectivity phrase and the list of sequences. Hua supplies a coherent condition testing exactness by objects of $P_0$, but its footnote says that the mutilated formulation has been restored. That reconstruction is a candidate mathematical interpretation, not additional legible manuscript text.''',r'''Context: this differs in evidential status from Hua, “Les conditions a) à e)”, condition b; it preserves that section as a guide for a later image review without altering any frozen reading.''',H1,r'mutil|restitu|conditions a|projectiv')
add('A10','Universal complexes and relative exactness','The upside-down cancelled sheet remains part of the record','059','whole cancelled draft','withdrawn in the same text',r'''The cancelled sheet begins “Il faut prouver” and includes a proposed admissible-kernel statement and a restriction equivalence between functor categories. Because these statements are struck out, they are evidence of an attempted route, not additional live hypotheses or established lemmas. Their relation to the revised intrinsic presentation on 058 and 060–062 should be studied as drafting history.''',r'''Context: Hua, “Les conditions a) à e)”, smooths this stretch into a live exposition; this page adds cancelled material absent from that modernised argument.''',H1,r'noyau|restriction|intrinsèque|conditions a')
add('A11','Universal complexes and relative exactness','The homology-free boundary separates P from Q','060–063','060 lines24–29;061 lines24–29;063 opening','own idea / reading question',r'''The image descriptions alternately omit or impose freeness of $H_i$, whereas 063 explicitly defines $Q$ by freeness of $L_i,Z_i,B_i,H_i$. Over $\mathbb Z$, the complex $\mathbb Z\xrightarrow{2}\mathbb Z$ is bounded finite free, with free cycles and boundaries but torsion homology; it belongs to the category defined on 049 and cannot be a sum of the elementary acyclic complexes and free homology objects. This tests both 060's omission of $H_i$ in the claimed image of $P_1$ and 061's apparent imposition of free homology on all of $P$.''',r'''Context: Hua, “Les conditions a) à e)”, explicitly reads the latter image as all of $\mathcal P$ and calls this a doubt; our calculation supports the need for that distinction while leaving the manuscript unchanged.''',H1,r'homologie|pas libre|image essentielle|torsion','High for the two-term counterexample; medium for assigning a single intended correction to the changing symbols.')
add('A12','Universal complexes and relative exactness','A kernel can be admissible without its image inclusion being admissible','062','top;lines3–16','own idea',r'''The warning “le mono $A'\longrightarrow B$ ne sera pas en général un $\Sigma$-mono” has a concrete explanation over a principal ideal domain: a degreewise image in a finite-free module is free, so the kernel sequence splits degreewise, but the subsequent inclusion can have torsion quotient. Already $2:\mathbb Z\to\mathbb Z$, placed in degree zero, has zero kernel and zero categorical cokernel in finite-free modules, yet is not invertible. This explains the deleted claim that the category is abelian and the surviving warning about bimorphisms.''',r'''Context: this adds an explicit test to Hua, “Les conditions a) à e)”, concluding kernel remark; the principal-ideal-domain restriction is retained rather than generalized silently.''',H1,r'bimorph|principal|noyau|conoyau','High in the stated finite-free PID setting; not asserted for arbitrary base rings.')
add('A13','Universal complexes and relative exactness','Two different roles for the elementary objects','063–064','063 opening;064 lines3–5','unpursued implication',r'''The manuscript distinguishes the injectives generated by the $C^i$ from projectives requiring the additional $\Psi^0$, warning that $K$ does not contain “assez” of the latter. This asymmetry matters when dualizing an argument or treating the monoidal unit as already present in the category of resolutions. It reappears concretely in the unit difficulty on 069.''',r'''Context: this supports Hua, “Algèbre homologique relative” and “L'unité, et l'extension aux foncteurs exacts à gauche”, while adding a cross-reference between the two uses of $\Psi^0$.''',H1+'; '+B6,r'assez de projectifs|injectifs|unité|Psi')
add('A14','Universal complexes and relative exactness','Comparison maps are unique as homotopy classes','063–064','063 middle–bottom;064 opening','unpursued implication',r'''The assertion “il y a une seule classe d'homotopie” concerns comparison maps between resolutions of the same object, with the augmentation fixed. It gives independence in the homotopy category, not a uniquely chosen chain map or all higher coherent choices. The usual relative comparison theorem is an appropriate proof model once admissibility, enough injectives and exactness assumptions have been established [B09, Theorem 12.4, and its dual].''',r'''Context: this restates Hua, “Algèbre homologique relative”, and adds the distinction relevant to PS VII §134's integrator choices and to the later product on 072.''',H1+'; MPL_136_047-094 §2 (first-pass lead)',r'homotopie|résolutions|integrator|comparaison')
add('A15','Universal complexes and relative exactness','Derived evaluation needs a target category in which it is defined','064–065','064 lines7–9;065 first line','unproved claim',r'''The phrases “on doit pouvoir montrer” and “sauf erreur” qualify the identification of the derived evaluation $F\mapsto F(\Psi^0)$ with the cohomology of $F(C^\bullet)$. A modern statement must specify the functor category's exact structure, the target's kernels/cohomology and the resolutions or derived category used. The displayed $RF(\Psi^0)\simeq F(C^\bullet)$ is a promising model for that proof, not a replacement for those hypotheses.''',r'''Context: Hua, “Algèbre homologique relative”, repeats the expected identification; this adds explicit foundational obligations and does not close unrelated open reconstruction items in RESULTS_STATE.''',H1,r'sauf erreur|dérivés|Gamma|évaluation')
add('A16','Universal complexes and relative exactness','A topical abbreviation should not become a topos by context','063','middle of long paragraph;TeX line3','reading question',r'''The frozen reading is “en top Algébrique”, in a sentence about cohomology computations, whereas Hua prints “en topos algébrique”. The abbreviation naturally permits a topical reading such as algebraic topology, but the image has not been reinterpreted after the freeze. This is a precise question for a future independent reader, not a correction.''',r'''Context: this differs from Hua, “Algèbre homologique relative”; no supplied reviewed result decides the letters.''',H1,r'topos algébrique|topologie|calcul|relative')

add('M01','Multiplicative structures and the de Rham comparison','The transported tensor product is specified before the cochain product','065–066','065 middle–bottom;066 opening','unpursued implication',r'''The product is defined “composante par composante” in the simplicial realization and transported with “notation $*$, pour éviter des confusions”. Accordingly, a monoidal claim about $*$ cannot simply be rewritten using the ordinary total tensor product of complexes. A comparison between those products, with its natural transformations and coherences, is part of any modern reconstruction of the argument.''',r'''Context: this supports Hua, “Le produit tensoriel des modèles”, and sharpens the warning in MPL_133 B06; PS VI §124, p. 521, separately distinguishes the semisimplicial-schematic approach from the divided-power de Rham approach.''',B6+'; '+WG,r'composante|confusion|multiplicative|124')
add('M02','Multiplicative structures and the de Rham comparison','The co-ideal sentence remains textually difficult','065','middle;TeX line13 and margin15','reading question',r'''The frozen text says “co-idéal” and leaves the explanatory clause concerning the other factor uncertain. Hua's “idéal tensoriel” describes the expected closure of the product when one factor is acyclic, but it is a mathematical interpretation of the sentence. Neither the ideal terminology nor the expected tensor argument should be substituted for the recorded ink.''',r'''Context: this differs from Hua, “Le produit tensoriel des modèles”; the comparison adds a localized question about the word and the object referred to by the final clause.''',H1+'; '+B6,r'idéal|acyclique|tensoriel|co-idéal')
add('M03','Multiplicative structures and the de Rham comparison','The definition gives symmetric lax monoidal data','066–067','066 lines5–13 and diagrams;067 top','unpursued implication',r'''“Structure multiplicative sur $F$” means maps $F(X)\otimes F(Y)\to F(X\otimes Y)$ and $1_B\to F(1_{B'})$ satisfying associativity, unit and symmetry diagrams. In modern terminology this is symmetric lax monoidal data on the additive functor; 067 explains the resulting preservation of ring objects. This identifies what the words mean locally, without asserting that the entire formalism has already been equated with a category of strictly commutative cosimplicial algebras.''',r'''Context: this supports the main interpretation in Hua, “Foncteurs multiplicatifs”, and supplies direct evidence for the B7 question in the context README, with the unit-strength reservation in M04.''',B6+'; '+WG,r'Foncteurs multiplicatifs|lax|cosimplicial|structure multiplicative')
add('M04','Multiplicative structures and the de Rham comparison','Hua strengthens the unit map','066','unit arrow;TeX line13','reading question',r'''The unit arrow $1_B\xrightarrow{\alpha}F(1_{B'})$ has no isomorphism sign in the frozen transcription, whereas the constraint arrows elsewhere on the page explicitly carry one. Hua calls this arrow an isomorphism and describes a functor “à unité forte”. That stronger hypothesis is not licensed by the recorded arrow alone and should be checked separately if used in a theorem.''',r'''Context: this differs from Hua, “Foncteurs multiplicatifs” (around lines1587–1593), and qualifies rather than rejects his lax-monoidal interpretation.''',B6+'; '+WG,r'unité forte|isomorphisme|alpha|Foncteurs multiplicatifs')
add('M05','Multiplicative structures and the de Rham comparison','The generator formulas encode more than one binary chain map','067–069','067 final sentence;068 displays;069 top','unpursued implication',r'''The name “str. mul. demi-simpliciale” is followed by bifunctor maps on the universal generators and compatibility with differential and half-cosimplicial operations. These formulas are a concrete place to seek a cosimplicial-algebra interpretation of the planning guess. Establishing that interpretation still requires a functor in both directions and a check of the unit and all stated compatibilities.''',r'''Context: this adds explicit scan-level evidence to MPL_133 B06 and Hua, “Foncteurs multiplicatifs”; PS VI §128, p. 540, supplies a later explicit model in “co-semisimplicial commutative algebras”, not an automatic identification of this construction.''',B6+'; '+WG,r'demi-simpliciale|bicomplexe|cosimplicial|128')
add('M06','Multiplicative structures and the de Rham comparison','The missing unit is acknowledged and repaired by enlarging the category','067;069–071','069 remark;071 unit paragraph and margin','withdrawn in the same text',r'''After applying the multiplicative-functor language to $K$ on 067, the manuscript observes “$K$ n'a pas d'unité pour $*$!” on 069. It passes to $P_0$ or $Q$ and ultimately describes a unit by a map into degree-zero cycles; the final margin on 071 suggests expressing it without requiring the cycle object itself to exist. Thus the unit issue is detected within the text, while the kernel-free reformulation is still only indicated.''',r'''Context: this supports Hua, “L'unité, et l'extension aux foncteurs exacts à gauche”; it adds the chronological correction to the broad definition on 067 and the link to the projective/injective distinction in A13.''',B6+'; '+H1,r'n.a pas d.unité|axiome d.unité|Ker|extension')
add('M07','Multiplicative structures and the de Rham comparison','A monomorphism proves uniqueness, not existence of the factorization','070–071','071 square and following paragraph','unproved claim / self-flagged',r'''The unknown arrow is literally marked “?” and the text argues that the right-hand vertical monomorphism gives its unique determination. That proves at most uniqueness if a factorization exists; existence requires showing that the lower composite lands in the indicated kernel or subobject. Independence of resolutions and the inherited coherence diagrams then need their own argument or a suitably functorial kernel construction.''',r'''Context: this supports the gap identified in Hua, “L'unité, et l'extension aux foncteurs exacts à gauche”, but does not endorse his further assertion that target tensor-flatness is necessary; M08 gives a more limited proposed repair.''',B6+'; '+H1,r'factorisation|platitude|indépendant|extension')
add('M08','Multiplicative structures and the de Rham comparison','A possible factorization proof using the source exact structure','071','square;TeX lines7–17','own idea',r'''Choose admissible injective presentations $X\hookrightarrow I^0\to I^1$ and $Y\hookrightarrow J^0\to J^1$, and suppose $X*Y$ is the admissible kernel of $I^0*J^0\to(I^1*J^0)\oplus(I^0*J^1)$ and $F$ preserves this kernel. Naturality of the product on injective objects makes the map $F(X)\otimes F(Y)\to F(I^0*J^0)$ vanish under both components, hence factor uniquely through $F(X*Y)$ using only biadditivity of the target tensor product at this step. Verifying that source-kernel presentation and its functoriality is the remaining task; this is not a proof of the whole extension theorem.''',r'''Context: this adds a conditional route to Hua, “L'unité, et l'extension aux foncteurs exacts à gauche”, and differs from a claim that preservation of kernels by $\otimes_B$ is already known to be necessary.''',B6+'; '+H1,r'noyau|platitude|extension|tensor','Medium: the kernel argument is formal under the stated assumptions, but those assumptions and full coherence have not been established here.')
add('M09','Multiplicative structures and the de Rham comparison','The resulting cup product has the stated laws up to homotopy','072','final paragraph;TeX lines16–20','unpursued implication',r'''The page says “le tout à homotopie près” after listing associativity, commutativity and the unit, with an explicit warning about signs. It therefore gives a product on cochains at that level and a graded algebra on cohomology, not an assertion of strict graded commutativity for the ordinary cochain product. A complete $E_\infty$ comparison would additionally identify compatible higher operations, for which [BF04, Theorem 2.1.1] is a modern reference model.''',r'''Context: this restates Hua, “Le cup-produit”, supports MPL_133 B06, and adds the precise boundary between individually stated homotopies and a coherent operadic structure.''',B6+'; '+WG,r'cup|homotopie|commutativité|E_')
add('M10','Multiplicative structures and the de Rham comparison','A minimal calculation separates cosimplicial and DGA commutativity','065–072','comparison with072 product','own idea',r'''On the oriented edge $[01]$, take a zero-cochain $f$ with $f(0)=0,f(1)=1$ and a one-cochain $e$ with $e([01])=1$. The Alexander–Whitney product gives $(f\smile e)([01])=0$ but $(e\smile f)([01])=1$, although the pointwise function rings in every simplicial degree are commutative [BF04, §2.2.8]. This tests a distinction of products, not a claim that the interval admits no other commutative model.''',r'''Context: this adds an elementary illustration to the MPL_133 B06 lead and to Hua, “Le cup-produit”; it does not change the manuscript's own product formulas.''',B6,r'commutativ|cup|cosimplicial|produit','High: direct evaluation of the two endpoint formulas; standard example, with no novelty claim.')
add('M11','Multiplicative structures and the de Rham comparison','The de Rham assertion is weightwise and truncated','073–074','073 middle–end;074 full sentence','unpursued implication',r'''The deleted “non tronqué” and the inserted “jusqu'au degré $n$ … incl.” give a material bound on the comparison. For each weight the manuscript describes a resolution with a final noninjective term and claims a homotopy comparison to a truncated cochain complex. No statement on these pages proves that all weights assemble into a space-reconstruction equivalence or that the characteristic-zero remark removes the bound.''',r'''Context: this supports Hua, “Le complexe de De Rham à puissances divisées”, and restates the kind of bounded additive comparison reported in MPL_136_189-234 §2; that earlier return is only a supplied lead, not a second checked proof here.''','MPL_136_189-234 §2 (first-pass lead); '+WG+'; WG2 §2.4 #5 (unchecked plan)',r'tronqué|poids|De Rham|136_189')
add('M12','Multiplicative structures and the de Rham comparison','Good truncation is a plausible interpretation; naive truncation is not interchangeable','073','last half of paragraph','own idea / reading question',r'''The phrase “complexe tronqué supérieur” and cohomology through degree $n$ fit a truncation ending in $Z^n(C)$, because the left-exact formula on 055 gives $F(\Psi^n)=Z^n(C)$. A naive truncation ending in all of $C^n$ generally changes $H^n$: the complex $k\xrightarrow{\mathrm{id}}k$ in degrees $n,n+1$ has zero $H^n$, while its naive upper truncation has $H^n=k$. This makes the exact truncation convention a substantive reading question, not harmless terminology.''',r'''Context: this differs from Hua's “tronqué naïf” in “Le complexe de De Rham à puissances divisées” and adds a countertest to the bounded comparison reported in MPL_136_189-234 §2.''','MPL_136_189-234 §2 (first-pass lead); '+WG,r'tronqué naïf|tronqué|Psi|cycles','High for the counterexample; medium for the intended manuscript truncation, since the frozen words remain unchanged.')
add('M13','Multiplicative structures and the de Rham comparison','The sign of the assertion about extra PD structure is a reading question','073','opening half of paragraph','reading question',r'''The frozen reading begins “On en peut affirmer encore” and later has an uncertain “naturel”, while Hua writes a negative assertion about what cannot yet be said without adding PD structure. These are materially different readings of a difficult sentence. The notes record the disagreement without using either version to repair the transcription or decide a theorem.''',r'''Context: this differs from Hua, “Le complexe de De Rham à puissances divisées”; its surrounding mathematically smoother account is not evidence that the negative wording is on the scan.''',WG,r'ne peut dire|structure p.d|stable|De Rham')
add('M14','Multiplicative structures and the de Rham comparison','Characteristic zero alone does not invert denominators','074','sole text;TeX line3','own idea / scope question',r'''The concluding phrase is “De Rham-Sullivan … Car. $0$”. If the proposed comparison uses division by all factorials, characteristic zero for an arbitrary ring is insufficient, since $\mathbb Z$ has characteristic zero but $2$ is not invertible. A precise theorem should distinguish a characteristic-zero field or a $\mathbb Q$-algebra from a general characteristic-zero coefficient ring.''',r'''Context: Hua, concluding sentence of “Le complexe de De Rham à puissances divisées”, repeats the characteristic-zero language; this adds a hypothesis check rather than proving that the author intended the broader ring claim.''','WG2 §2.4 #5 (unchecked plan); '+WG,r'caractéristique zéro|caractéristique 0|Sullivan|dénominateur','High for the arithmetic distinction; medium for its relevance to the intended unstated theorem.')

add('P01','Polynomial categories and divided powers','Additivity is deliberately one-sided','075;083;086','075 middle;083 composition;086 lines6–10','unpursued implication',r'''The boxed formula $(f+g)\circ h=f\circ h+g\circ h$ is followed by the explicit failure of distributivity in the other variable. Thus the polynomial category has abelian groups of maps but is not being asserted to be a preadditive category under its full composition. Any reconstruction from $\operatorname{Hom}(\Gamma^nX,Y)$ must preserve this asymmetry.''',r'''Context: this supports Hua, “Axiomes” and “La réciproque”, and supplies a precise qualification of the polynomial-category candidate in RESULTS_STATE.''',H2,r'additive en|distributivité|polynomiale|réciproque')
add('P02','Polynomial categories and divided powers','The scalar-action formula contains a type question','075–076','075 final display;076 first display','reading question',r'''The last formula on 075 reads $\mu_n(f)=f\circ(n\operatorname{id}_Y)$ for $f:X\to Y$, while the homogeneous formula on 076 uses $\operatorname{id}_X$. With the stated direction of $f$, precomposition requires the source identity. Hua makes that correction, but the mismatch is retained in the diplomatic text and recorded here instead.''',r'''Context: this differs from the corrected formula in Hua, “Axiomes”; the next scan itself supplies the competing source-index form.''',H2,r'mu|id.*X|id.*Y|homogénéité')
add('P03','Polynomial categories and divided powers','The author retracts scalar recognition of the grading','076–077','076 remark and margin;077 opening','withdrawn in the same text',r'''The cancelled uniqueness proof is contradicted by the author's own example that on a group killed by a prime $p$, $\lambda$ and $\lambda^p$ act alike. Scan 077 therefore makes the decomposition “structure supplémentaire” and considers compatibility over all base algebras. The resulting axiom system includes chosen degree data; it does not recover degrees from the action of the integer scalars alone.''',r'''Context: this restates Hua, “Axiomes”, and adds direct manuscript support to the arithmetic caution in MPL_133 B01 without claiming that the latter lead concerns this exact counterexample.''',H2+'; MPL_133 B01 (first-pass lead)',r'canular|arithméti|structure supplémentaire|unique')
add('P04','Polynomial categories and divided powers','Polynomial laws are distinguished from functions by base extension','076–077;086','077 first paragraph;086 last sentence','own idea',r'''Over $\mathbb F_p$, the functions $x\mapsto x$ and $x\mapsto x^p$ agree on the base field, but their extensions evaluated at $t\in\mathbb F_p[t]$ differ. This explains why the proposed all-algebras test on 077 and “non seulement comme application ensembliste” on 086 belong together. It is a test for lost structure, not proof that the stated categorical axioms already enforce all base-change compatibilities.''',r'''Context: this adds an elementary example to Hua, “Axiomes” and “Questions ouvertes”; [FS97, §2, discussion preceding Definition 2.1] makes the related distinction between polynomial maps and their values on a finite field explicit.''',H2+'; '+WG,r'extension|ensembliste|polynomial|Friedlander','High: evaluate the two maps at an indeterminate; this is a standard finite-field example.')
add('P05','Polynomial categories and divided powers','Multilinear representability needs more than scalar weights','077–078','077 axioms e–g;078 lines5–14','unproved claim',r'''The passage calls $(X,Y;Z)\mapsto P_{(1,1)}(X,Y;Z)$ “triadditif” and then represents it by $X\otimes Y$. A full derivation should check additivity under addition maps, compatibility of multidegrees with regrouping variables and the degree-one subcategory, rather than infer multilinearity from integer weights alone. Those requirements are particularly visible after the arithmetic failure on 076.''',r'''Context: this adds proof obligations to Hua, “Produits tensoriels et puissances divisées”, and to the unreviewed equivalence candidate; its homogeneous-core formalization does not cover these product and multidegree axioms.''',H2,r'triadditif|multidegré|produits tensoriels|formalisés')
add('P06','Polynomial categories and divided powers','Strict representability is a parameter-stability condition','079','top half;lines3–19','unpursued implication',r'''“On est obligé de renforcer la propriété universelle” leads to the requirement that substitution through $X\times Y\to T$ preserve maps with additional variables and degrees. Here “strict” is a stronger representability property, not an assertion that associativity constraint maps are identity maps. A Yoneda proof of the associator should use the same representing functor with every extra parameter retained.''',r'''Context: this restates Hua, “Produits tensoriels et puissances divisées”, and sharpens the meaning of “strict” in the polynomial-category candidate.''',H2,r'strict|représentabilité|param|associativité')
add('P07','Polynomial categories and divided powers','The proposed tensor unit acquires a proof only under stronger assumptions','078–080','078 final questions;079 unit question;080 lines15–20','self-flagged question / resolved conditionally',r'''The questions whether $1=\Gamma^0(0)$ satisfies $1\otimes X\simeq X$ are explicit on 078–079. Scan 080 supplies an argument using the assumed exponential decomposition and the degree-$(1,0)$ representing property. Thus the later conclusion is conditional on the added isomorphism hypotheses, not a consequence established from bare representability on 078.''',r'''Context: this supports Hua, “Produits tensoriels et puissances divisées”, and adds the dependency of the resolved question on the strengthened axioms.''',H2,r'unité|Gamma.*0|exponentielle|stricts')
add('P08','Polynomial categories and divided powers','Karoubian splitting addresses existence of multihomogeneous summands','079–080','079 last paragraph;080 first displays','unpursued implication',r'''The parenthesis “automatique si $P_1$ est karoubienne” concerns representing summand functors, while the map from a multihomogeneous representing object to the tensor of the $\Gamma^{\nu_i}X_i$ is subsequently assumed to be an isomorphism. Splitting idempotents can supply the summands when the homogeneous decomposition gives the requisite projections; it does not itself prove that additional tensor comparison is an isomorphism. This separates two existence claims that a compressed exposition could merge.''',r'''Context: this restates and separates the stages in Hua, “Produits tensoriels et puissances divisées”, and adds an explicit dependency to the candidate equivalence.''',H2,r'karoub|représentab|exponentielle|isomorphisme')
add('P09','Polynomial categories and divided powers','An exponential functor suggests a Hopf structure','080–081','080 exponential formula;081 opening','own idea / unpursued implication',r'''The text says that $\Gamma^*$ transforms sums into tensor products and thereby obtains a commutative graded algebra. Under the stated coherent symmetric-monoidal hypotheses, addition, diagonal, zero and negation on $X$ also suggest multiplication, comultiplication, unit/counit and antipode on $\Gamma^*(X)$. Checking their identities is a useful algebraic extension of the page, with polynomial weight distinguished from a cohomological grading carrying Koszul signs.''',r'''Context: this adds a conditional Hopf-algebra comparison to Hua, “Produits tensoriels et puissances divisées”; PS VI §120's discussion of additive and multiplicative diagonals provides a related later context, not a proof of equivalence of the frameworks.''',H2+'; '+WG,r'exponentielle|diagon|Hopf|analyseur','High under a coherent symmetric strong-monoidal functor; no claim that all required compatibilities are proved in these scans.')
add('P10','Polynomial categories and divided powers','The homogeneous composition core has a clear categorical shape','081–083','081 alpha;082 unit diagrams;083 composition','own idea / unpursued implication',r'''The formula $g\circ f=g\,\Gamma^q(f)\alpha_{q,p}$ uses maps $\Gamma^{pq}X\to\Gamma^p\Gamma^qX$ with coassociativity and degree-one identities. It has the shape of a homogeneous co-Kleisli construction for a comonad graded by the multiplicative degree monoid. That interpretation isolates a manageable core, but does not automatically construct sums of different-degree arrows, products, all multidegrees or the tensor-PD equivalence.''',r'''Context: this supports the limited core described in Hua, “La réciproque” and its Lean footnote; its name “Kleisli” is interpreted by the displayed arrow direction, not used to import a monad of the opposite variance.''',H2,r'comonade|Kleisli|alpha|formalisés','High for the direction and homogeneous composition pattern; a full categorical comparison remains unproved here.')
add('P11','Polynomial categories and divided powers','The alpha square has a source-index reading issue','081','associativity square;TeX lines20–23','reading question',r'''The right-hand label retains the uncertain superscript $\Gamma^r(X)$ in the frozen text. The typed map required by that side of the square is $\Gamma^p(\alpha^X_{q,r})$, as Hua writes; the bottom map instead legitimately uses $\alpha^{\Gamma^r(X)}_{p,q}$. This identifies why the source index matters without substituting a type-correct label for uncertain ink.''',r'''Context: this differs from Hua, “Produits tensoriels et puissances divisées”, which corrects the square; the transcription's doubt remains in place.''',H2,r'alpha|associativité|composition|exposant')
add('P12','Polynomial categories and divided powers','Degree zero is a separate unit condition and an acknowledged omission','082–083','082 u0;083 circled arrow and final sentence','self-flagged omission / supplied in the converse',r'''The isomorphism $1\to\Gamma^q(1)$ is distinguished from the degree-one identity conditions, and 083 says “Je l'avais oublié dans les axiomes préliminaires il me semble”. This is a correction of the axiom list, not an automatic consequence of $\Gamma^1=\mathrm{id}$. Any full equivalence statement must include constants and check this extra condition.''',r'''Context: this supports Hua, “La réciproque”; its formalization footnote explicitly excludes $1\simeq\Gamma^q(1)$, so the reported homogeneous result does not close this obligation.''',H2,r'oublié|Gamma.*1|constantes|formalisés')
add('P13','Polynomial categories and divided powers','A proposed bilinearity reading is not in the frozen text','075;083','075 distributivity formulas;083 lines7–15','reading question',r'''Scan 083 is transcribed as “des applications”, while Hua's footnote proposes “applications bil.” and marks that reading uncertain. Bilinearity in both homogeneous inputs would conflict in general with the explicit one-sided rule on075 and the nonlinear dependence of $\Gamma^q(f)$ on $f$. The research comparison therefore records both the textual discrepancy and the reason not to turn it into an additional axiom.''',r'''Context: this differs from the tentative phrase in Hua, “La réciproque”, footnote at lines1879–1882, while agreeing with his explanation of outer-variable additivity.''',H2,r'applications bil|additive en|Gamma|réciproque')
add('P14','Polynomial categories and divided powers','The announced equivalence is broader than the verified homogeneous core','084','lines3–6','unproved claim',r'''“On vérifie alors les axiomes c) à g)” and “Les deux notions sont donc en équivalence” compress several nontrivial checks. A complete proof must define composition of arbitrary finite sums, handle mixed terms and constants, and show compatibility of $\alpha$ with the exponential and multidegree structures so that the tensor universal properties survive. Those checks cannot be inferred merely from associativity of homogeneous composition.''',r'''Context: this supports Hua's description of an asserted equivalence but limits the candidate 137-polynomial-categories-pd-tensor-categories to what is actually supplied; Hua's explicit Lean exclusions leave precisely many of these parts open in that report.''',H2,r'cœur|équivalence|Ne sont pas formalisés|exponentielle')
add('P15','Polynomial categories and divided powers','The later PD question asks for maps in the opposite direction','081–082;085–086','085 final question;086 opening','unpursued implication',r'''The earlier $\alpha$ maps run from $\Gamma^{pq}M$ to $\Gamma^p\Gamma^qM$, whereas the question “quand et comment” on 085 asks for a PD structure producing maps in the reverse direction. These are different pieces of structure, so the name “$\otimes$-pd-catégorie” does not itself answer the question. Scan 086 explicitly says a canonical structure exists in the module example, leaving the abstract existence problem separate.''',r'''Context: this supports the abstract question in Hua, “Questions ouvertes”, but qualifies his module-case “Réponse nôtre”, since canonicity in the example is already asserted on 086; his added explanation supplies the positive-degree restriction discussed in P17.''',H2+'; '+WG,r'Questions ouvertes|puissances divisées|canonique|Gamma')
add('P16','Polynomial categories and divided powers','The two canonical maps need not be inverses','081;085–086','alpha construction;PD question and example','own idea',r'''For $M=\mathbb Zx$ and $p,q\geq1$, write $e_n=\gamma_n^M(x)$ and $u_{p,q}=\gamma_p^{\Gamma^qM}(e_q)$: the representing map has $\alpha_{p,q}(e_{pq})=u_{p,q}$. Standard PD evaluation in the reverse direction sends $u_{p,q}$ to $\frac{(pq)!}{p!(q!)^p}e_{pq}$, so for $p=q=2$ its composite with $\alpha$ is $3\,\mathrm{id}$ [Stacks,07GL(5)]. The map $\alpha$ itself is an isomorphism in this rank-one case; the example shows that its inverse is not the standard PD evaluation.''',r'''Context: this adds a calculation to Hua, “Produits tensoriels et puissances divisées” and “Questions ouvertes”, distinguishing the two canonical constructions without contradicting the existence asserted on 086.''',H2+'; '+WG,r'composition|Gamma|p.d.|Questions ouvertes','High: both modules are free of rank one and the PD iteration coefficient is 3; no novelty claim.')
add('P17','Polynomial categories and divided powers','The PD operations require attention to degree zero','085–086','085 reverse maps;086 canonical example','own idea / scope question',r'''The usual divided powers on $\Gamma^*(M)$ are defined on its positive-degree augmentation ideal, so the reverse construction in P16 uses $q\geq1$. Requiring the usual PD axioms on the unit ideal of $\mathbb Z$, or on all of $\Gamma_{\mathbb Z}(M)$ would give $2\gamma_2(1)=1$, which is impossible. The abstract question should therefore specify its ideal and index range rather than conflate the degree-zero polynomial-category unit condition with PD operations on the unit.''',r'''Context: this adds a scope check to the question on 085; Hua's module-case answer explicitly inserts $q\geq1$, and PS VI §120, p.506, likewise locates PD operations on a suitable ideal.''',H2+'; MPL_133 B05 (first-pass lead)',r'q.*1|suitable ideal|idéal|Questions ouvertes','High for the integral obstruction; this does not claim that the manuscript intended divided powers of the unit.')
add('P18','Polynomial categories and divided powers','The graded-module example needs a symmetry convention','085','example paragraph and question mark;lines7–10','own idea / unproved claim',r'''The example places $\Gamma^n(M^i)$ in degree $ni$ but does not spell out the symmetry convention in the surviving lines. An ordinary weight grading and a cohomological grading with Koszul symmetry impose different commutativity conditions on odd-degree elements, so this should be fixed before treating the graded example as a theorem. The adjacent question mark is preserved as a question about this passage, without assigning it a more precise target than the page establishes.''',r'''Context: Hua, “La réciproque”, module example, repeats the graded construction; this adds a convention check, not a counterexample to a fully specified statement.''',H2,r'gradués|degré ni|symétrie|Modules','Medium: the need to specify symmetry is certain, but the intended convention is not fixed by this excerpt.')
add('P19','Polynomial categories and divided powers','The final question is enrichment on Hom modules','086','last two displays;lines8–10','unpursued implication',r'''The requirement is that $f\mapsto\Gamma^p(f)$ be an “application pol. de degré $p$” between Hom modules, “et non seulement comme application ensembliste”. This asks for structured polynomial dependence on morphisms, beyond the existence of the object functors $\Gamma^p$. Friedlander–Suslin [FS97, Definition 2.1] is a close modern comparison, but its finite-dimensional vector-space setting should not be called literally identical to this arbitrary-$k$ categorical question.''',r'''Context: this supports the direction of Hua, “Questions ouvertes”, while qualifying his “mot pour mot” comparison; Roby [R63] is a bibliographic lead supplied by Hua, not an author named on these scans.''',H2+'; '+WG,r'ensembliste|Friedlander|mot pour mot|Roby')

add('R01','The binomial bridge, Witt and the planning questions','These pages do not establish integral reconstruction','063–086','relative resolutions, multiplicative application, final questions','unpursued implication / bounded conclusion',r'''The excerpt provides universal cochain functors, multiplicative data, a product “à homotopie près”, a weightwise truncated de Rham comparison and an asserted equivalence between polynomial-category structures. It does not prove that this package determines an integral homotopy type, nor construct the derived comparison with binomial cochains needed to deduce that conclusion by the proposed binomial route. Thus the planning guess is productive as a research direction, but not an established theorem of these 42 pages.''',r'''Context: this qualifies the B7 proposal in the supplied README and Hua's whole-cote “Résumé”; it adds locally checked boundaries to the leads MPL_136_047-094 §2, MPL_136_189-234 §2 and MPL_136_235-281 §86, whose full reports were not supplied.''',WG+'; MPL_133 B05/B06 (first-pass leads)',r'B7|homotopy|homotopie|136_235|reconstruction')
add('R02','The binomial bridge, Witt and the planning questions','The later failure is a particular comparison, not a rejection of every PD construction','073–074','de Rham example;comparison with PS119','unpursued implication / contextual comparison',r'''The supplied PS VI §119 records Illusie's objection to a proposed divided-power-derived-functor description of Eilenberg–MacLane homology, then gives a degree-two central-extension obstruction. Its splitting equation $P(a+a')-P(a)-P(a')=aa'$ has the integer-valued solution $P(a)=\binom a2$, but no solution in $\mathbb Z[a]$. This distinguishes a failure of that later schematic comparison from the bounded additive assertion actually written on 137/073.''',r'''Context: this supports and makes explicit the MPL_133 B05 lead using the supplied primary text itself, PS VI §119, pp. 500–503, rather than relying on the unavailable earlier note as a result.''','MPL_133 B05 (first-pass lead); '+WG,r'Illusie|W_2|comparison|binomial')
add('R03','The binomial bridge, Witt and the planning questions','The actual binomial comparison in the supplied later text is a question','073;075–086','comparison with PS119 p.503','unpursued implication',r'''PS VI §119 asks about the map from binomial polynomial cochains to all integer-valued cochains on degreewise finite-free models, under $H_0(L_*)=0$, possibly also $H_1(L_*)=0$. That is a more specific bridge than merely observing divided powers in137: it asks whether enlarging the permitted functions gives a quasi-isomorphism. The question's status and hypotheses must remain visible when connecting it to later theorems.''',r'''Context: this restates PS VI §119, pp. 502–504, and supports the direction of MPL_133 B05; it adds a precise later target to the cochain and polynomial-operation constructions in the present scans.''','MPL_133 B05 (first-pass lead); '+WG,r'Sym.*bin|H_0|H_1|binomial|503')
add('R04','The binomial bridge, Witt and the planning questions','Toën gives a qualified modern reconstruction target','065–074','external comparison with the multiplicative/cochain programme','unpursued implication / literature comparison',r'''For simply connected finite-type spaces, Toën's 2020 affinization theorem gives homotopy sheaves $\pi_i(X^{\mathrm{aff}})\simeq\pi_i(X)\otimes\mathbb H$, a functorial retraction on derived integral points, faithfulness and recognition of object classes; it does not give full faithfulness [T20, Théorème 0.1 and §5]. The binomial coordinate algebra of $\mathbb H$ makes it relevant, but none of those conclusions follows merely from the word “multiplicative” on 066.''',r'''Context: this adds a primary-source comparison to the README's Toën/B7 prompt and qualifies the whole-cote programme in Hua's “Résumé”.''',WG,r'Toën|Toen|binomial|B7|schématisation')
add('R05','The binomial bridge, Witt and the planning questions','Horel supplies a stronger result under explicit hypotheses','065–074;085–086','external comparison;no claim of a theorem on these scans','unpursued implication / literature comparison',r'''Horel's derived unit $X\to\mathbf R\operatorname{Map}_{c\mathrm{BRing}}(\mathbb Z^X,\mathbb Z)$ is an equivalence for connected nilpotent finite-type spaces [H24, Theorem 5.4]. Without nilpotence, Theorem 6.1 identifies the Bousfield–Kan integral-completion map for connected finite-type spaces. These are comparison targets, not conclusions proved by the present manuscript excerpt.''',r'''Context: this adds verified hypotheses to the README's Horel/B7 question and the source lead MPL_136_235-281 §86.''',WG+'; MPL_136_235-281 §86 (first-pass lead)',r'Horel|B7|binomial|136_235')
add('R06','The binomial bridge, Witt and the planning questions','The derived ambient category matters even when ordinary operations are determined','066–072;086','external comparison with enriched cochains','unpursued implication',r'''The ordinary fully faithful inclusion $c\mathrm{BRing}\to c\mathrm{Ring}$ does not remain fully faithful after localization at quasi-isomorphisms: Horel explicitly distinguishes the relevant replacements and derived mapping spaces [H24, Remarks 3.11, 8.5]. This is why adding the label “binomial” to an unchanged ordinary cochain object is not itself a proof of the desired homotopy-theoretic comparison.''',r'''Context: this adds a categorical boundary to the B7 proposal and to the final enrichment question on 086; PS VI §120's separate operation frameworks already caution against identifying structures by their names.''',WG+'; MPL_133 B06 (first-pass lead)',r'binomial|enrich|application|opérations')
add('R07','The binomial bridge, Witt and the planning questions','Recognition of spaces is not the same as full faithfulness on maps','072–073','external comparison with the stated homotopy-level product','unpursued implication / literature comparison',r'''Mandell's integral $E_\infty$ cochains recognize finite-type nilpotent spaces up to weak equivalence, while the cochain functor is faithful but not full [M06, Main Theorem and Theorems 0.1–0.2]. This supplies an established benchmark for what extra structure can do, but it does not identify the particular structure on 137/066–073 with a complete $E_\infty$ model.''',r'''Context: this adds a distinction between recognition and full faithfulness to the README's reconstruction question and the literature lead MPL_136_235-281 §86.''',WG+'; MPL_133 B06 (first-pass lead)',r'E_.*infty|Mandell|reconstitution|homotopie')
add('R08','The binomial bridge, Witt and the planning questions','The specified geometric examples are not named in the excerpt','045–086','whole-excerpt check;positive examples072–074,085–086','unpursued implication / bounded negative finding',r'''No abelian variety, Fourier transform or Chern class is named in the frozen 137/045–086 text. The named examples instead include the cup product, the divided-power de Rham complex, “De Rham-Sullivan” and modules on a ringed topos, including graded modules. The general topos example permits possible later geometric specializations, but those are not named examples on these scans.''',r'''Context: this does not confirm WG2 §2.4 #5; the planning README names the Fourier/Chern question, while PS VI §128, p. 541, gives the separate example of “an elliptic curve minus a point”, which does not occur in these 42 scans.''','WG2 §2.4 #5 (unchecked plan)',r'Fourier|Chern|abelian variet|variétés? abélien|variété abélien|elliptic|elliptique')
add('R09','The binomial bridge, Witt and the planning questions','Cote133 cannot be identified afresh from absent scans','045–086','whole excerpt;contextual133/006–009 only through the register','reading question / evidence boundary',r'''No “Witt”, “Burnside” or “binomial” occurs in the frozen 137 excerpt, and the supplied package does not contain the cote133 images or complete earlier notes. RESULTS_STATE reports “Explicitly quadratic Witt theory” on 133/006–009 and separately mentions finite-set operations. Therefore the initial finite-$G$-set/Witt-vector guess must be qualified by that reported quadratic meaning, while its exact manuscript relationship remains unverified here.''',r'''Context: this differs from a single undifferentiated Witt-vector reading and reconciles the truncated leads MPL_133 §2 and §88 with the titles B01/B05/B06, without promoting any of them to a reviewed result.''','MPL_133 §2,§88,B01 (first-pass leads); '+WG,r'Witt|Burnside|MPL_133|B01')
add('R10','The binomial bridge, Witt and the planning questions','The finite-set/Witt-vector bridge requires completion and a specified group','075–086','possible external comparison;not a construction named here','unpursued implication / literature comparison',r'''Dress–Siebeneicher identify the big Witt ring of $\mathbb Z$ with the completed Burnside ring of almost-finite sets acted on by the infinite cyclic group; finite cyclic sets give a proper dense subring [DS89, §1 and Theorem (5–4–2)]. In the profinite formulation, $\widehat{\mathbb Z}$ gives big Witt vectors and $\mathbb Z_p$ the $p$-typical case [Mi14, introduction]. Witt groups of forms, for example the symmetric spaces modulo metabolic relations in [Ba05, Definition 1.1.27], are a different construction.''',r'''Context: this adds a precise external comparison to WG2 §Week #3 and qualifies the interpretation of MPL_133 §2/§88; none of these literature results attributes a Witt-vector construction to the unseen 133 pages.''',WG+'; MPL_133 §2/§88 (first-pass leads)',r'Witt|finite|Burnside|quadratic')
add('R11','The binomial bridge, Witt and the planning questions','A Burnside ring need not itself be binomial','076–077','arithmetic degree warning;comparison with MPL_133 B01','own idea',r'''Let $t$ denote the free orbit of $C_2$; orbit multiplication gives $A(C_2)=\mathbb Z[t]/(t^2-2t)$ with basis $1,t$ [L05, Definition 1.1, Example 1.4]. Then $\binom t2=t/2$ is not in $A(C_2)$, although cardinality sends $t$ to 2 in the binomial ring $\mathbb Z$. This prevents transferring binomiality from a numerical rank target to an arbitrary ring of finite group-sets.''',r'''Context: this adds an elementary test to the title of MPL_133 B01 and agrees with PS VI §120's distinction of operation structures, without claiming to identify the missing 133 rank map.''','MPL_133 B01 (first-pass lead); '+WG,r'binomial|rank target|rang|finite','High: the two orbit classes give an integral basis and the product relation is explicit; standard calculation, not a novelty claim.')
add('R12','The binomial bridge, Witt and the planning questions','A divided-power algebra need not be binomial','085–086','module example and canonical PD structure','own idea',r'''In the free divided-power algebra $\Gamma_{\mathbb Z}(\mathbb Zx)$, $x^2=2\gamma_2(x)$ but $\binom x2=\gamma_2(x)-x/2$ is not integral in the divided-power basis. Conversely, the binomial ring $\mathbb Z$ cannot have ordinary divided powers on its entire unit ideal, by the calculation in P17. These two tests show why a substantive construction is needed to connect the manuscript's PD structures with binomial cochains.''',r'''Context: this adds concrete distinctions to PS VI §120, pp. 506–507, and to the B01/B05 leads, while respecting the module example on 085.''','MPL_133 B01/B05 (first-pass leads); '+WG,r'binomial|divided power|puissances divisées|ideal','High: the homogeneous integral basis detects the first obstruction and the unit identity detects the second; no novelty claim.')
add('R13','The binomial bridge, Witt and the planning questions','A restricted filtered bridge can be proved directly','076–086','own comparison with homogeneous operations','own idea',r'''Filter $\operatorname{Int}(\mathbb Z)$ by polynomial degree: Newton expansion has integral coefficients $(\Delta^nf)(0)$, so the binomial polynomials $b_n(t)=\binom tn$ form a basis with $\deg b_n=n$. Leading terms give $\overline b_m\,\overline b_n=\binom{m+n}{m}\overline b_{m+n}$ and hence $\operatorname{gr}_{\deg}\operatorname{Int}(\mathbb Z)\simeq\Gamma_{\mathbb Z}(\mathbb Zx)$ as ordinary commutative, weight-graded algebras. This is a precise rank-one bridge, with lower-degree terms deliberately discarded, not an equivalence of the homotopy theories in question.''',r'''Context: this adds a directly checked comparison to PS VI §119–120's binomial operations and to Hua's homogeneous $\Gamma^*$ framework; it does not settle WG2 §Week #3.''',WG+'; '+H2,r'binomial|homogène|Gamma|exponentielle','High for the basis and associated-graded calculation; standard observation, not claimed new.')
add('R14','The binomial bridge, Witt and the planning questions','Constants show what the associated graded forgets','077;082–083;086','constant maps, degree-zero axioms, final enrichment','own idea',r'''The polynomials $b=\binom t2$ and $c=b+t$ have the same degree-two symbol, but $b(1)=0$ and $c(1)=1$, so composition with a constant does not descend from these leading symbols alone. For positive-degree univariate polynomials, degree does multiply and leading composition is defined; specifically $\sigma_{mn}(b_m\circ b_n)=\frac{(mn)!}{m!(n!)^m}\overline b_{mn}$, matching PD iteration. A filtered lift must therefore reconstruct the discarded lower terms and check constants, units, substitution and eventually derived coherence, rather than rely only on R13.''',r'''Context: this adds a concrete obstruction to a too-quick bridge from Hua's homogeneous composition core to PS VI §120's analyzer operations, and makes the omission noticed on 083 relevant to the proposed comparison.''',WG+'; '+H2,r'constantes|oublié|analyseur|composition','High for the displayed computations; medium for the proposed research route, whose categorical and derived compatibilities are unproved.')
add('R15','The binomial bridge, Witt and the planning questions','The later analyzer discussion supplies a comparison problem, not an identification','081–086','composition and enrichment questions;PS120','unpursued implication',r'''The emphasis on composition and polynomial dependence makes a comparison with theories of operations more precise than a comparison of underlying graded modules alone. PS VI §120 distinguishes PD, lambda, operations of Adams type and binomial operations and discusses two diagonals; Borger–Wieland's binomial plethory [BW05, §2.14] supplies one modern comparison target. No evidence supplied here identifies the unseen cote153 analyzer with that particular plethory.''',r'''Context: this adds a bounded link between the present pages and the README's joint133/137/153 programme, while retaining the latter as an unchecked plan.''',WG+'; '+H2,r'analyseur|analyzer|153|pleth|binomial')
add('R16','The binomial bridge, Witt and the planning questions','A focused next theorem should keep its missing data explicit','065–086','all multiplicative and polynomial constructions','own idea / research proposal',r'''A concrete next task is to construct a comparison functor from a precisely specified version of the manuscript's structured cochains to a modern cosimplicial or binomial model, preserving units, substitutions and weak equivalences. The tests M08, M12, P14 and R14 identify separate places where a proof needs additional work, rather than a change of terminology. Even success there would still require the appropriate connectivity, nilpotence and finiteness hypotheses before applying a reconstruction theorem.''',r'''Context: this adds a work programme under WG2 §Week #3 and the two Hua candidates; it neither reproves any supplied settled result nor closes WG H6, whose separate finite-dimensional-local-system characterization is not addressed by these scans.''',WG+'; WG H6 (unchecked hunch); '+H1+'; '+H2,r'Week #3|WG H6|137-perfect|137-polynomial|B7','Medium: the proposed comparisons are well motivated by the displayed constructions, but no full functor or equivalence is constructed here.')
